#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189413 :tutorial/content "Given two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ the variance of the sum or difference of these variables is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X\\pm Y] = \\textrm{Var}[X] + \\textrm{Var}[Y] \\pm 2\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y],\n$$\n\nwhere $\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y]$ is the covariance of $X$ and $Y.$ We'll prove this formula at the end of the lesson.\n\nNote that if $X$ and $Y$ are independent, then $\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y]= 0,$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X\\pm Y] = \\textrm{Var}[X] + \\textrm{Var}[Y].\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "84bffdf5-551d-52b7-93f3-5c7ba599e972" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6496 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189414 :tutorial/content "If $X$ and $Y$ are random variables and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then \n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[aX + bY] = a^2 \\textrm{Var}[X] + b^2 \\textrm{Var}[Y] + 2ab \\textrm{Cov}[X,Y].\n$$\n\nIf $X$ and $Y$ are independent, then $\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y] = 0,$ and we recover the formula\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[aX + bY] = a^2 \\textrm{Var}[X] + b^2 \\textrm{Var}[Y] .\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "a2319f43-f4e9-5092-93cf-5e5f4f23f4f7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6502 :tutorial/title "The Variance of a Sum of Scaled Random Variables"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189420 :tutorial/content "Finally, we have the following generalization for the variance of a sum of $n$ random variables.\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var} \\left[ \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^{n} a_i X_i \\right] = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^{n} a_i^2 \\textrm{Var}[X_i] + 2\\sum\\limits_{i < j} a_i a_j \\textrm{Cov}[X_i, X_j]\n$$\n\nLet's see an example of how this works in practice." :tutorial/id #uuid "5169e0a6-5da8-51b4-8e84-5154fe8622f5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6535 :tutorial/title "Generalizing to Multiple Random Variables"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189421 :tutorial/content "Let's prove the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X+ Y] = \\textrm{Var}[X] + \\textrm{Var}[Y] + 2\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y]\n$$\n\nApplying the definition of variance and using the linearity of expectation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X+Y] & =\\text{E}[((X+Y)-\\text{E}[X+Y])^{2}] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[((X-\\text{E}[X])+(Y-\\text{E}[Y]))^{2}] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[(X-\\text{E}[X])^{2}+(Y-\\text{E}[Y])^{2}+2(X-\\text{E}[X])(Y-\\text{E}[Y])].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDistributing the expected value, and using the fact that $\\textrm{E}[aX] = a\\cdot \\textrm{E}[X]$ for constant $a,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X+Y] & =\\text{E}[(X-\\text{E}[X])^{2}+(Y-\\text{E}[Y])^{2}+2(X-\\text{E}[X])(Y-\\text{E}[Y])] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[(X-\\text{E}[X])^{2}]+\\text{E}[(Y-\\text{E}[Y])^{2}]+2\\cdot \\text{E}[(X-\\text{E}[X])(Y-\\text{E}[Y])] \\\\  & =\\text{Var}[X]+\\text{Var}[Y]+2\\text{Cov}[X,Y]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required. \n\nThe proof for the difference between two random variables is similar." :tutorial/id #uuid "7523c9e9-51f7-52f5-b206-eec1940bf0f9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6536 :tutorial/title "Proof of the Sum Formula"}]}
